{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01YQDJUNLGpwv2WwGWqCqG6f","type":"tool_result","content":"1\tPAYLOAD 7/32. Files covered: _lib/_ceo_boot_parts/telemetry_candidates.py, _lib/_ceremony_toolkit/{__init__,bundle_builder,preflight}.py, _lib/_contamination/{__init__,cli_checks,personal_constants,personal_facade_io,personal_path_policy,personal_path_rows,personal_path_scanner,personal_reporting,term_scan}.py, _lib/_context_budget_parts/{__init__,compaction,inventory}.py. All are new files. Most are code moved out of check_contamination.py, ceo-boot.py and context-budget.py.\n2\t\n3\tWHAT I CHECKED AND FOUND SOUND\n4\t- Contamination facade binding (.claude/scripts/check_contamination.py:402-506). Every global that the extracted functions use resolves in the facade namespace. That covers constants copied from personal_constants, the policy-only regexes exported through __all__, _PERSONAL_PATH_NAME_LINE from the scanner, and stdlib modules imported by the facade. The order of personal_path_verdict is correct: the policy implementation becomes _personal_path_verdict_impl, then the wrapper is published. NamedTuple and exception classes keep one identity. The default root in main() uses the facade's __file__, so the result is the same as before. Patch points survive: FileWalker and _personal_path_index_modes are looked up at call time, and term_scan receives its dependencies as arguments.\n5\t- ceo-boot parts run inside the facade dict under their real filenames. Every name telemetry_candidates.py uses exists at that point (_rp, typing, resource, tracemalloc, AUDIT_LOG_DEFAULT, _audit_emit, TIER_*_CHECKS, dispatch_parallel).\n6\t- context-budget install(): deepcopy and rebinding are safe for the inventory and compaction declarations. CompactionPolicy is rebuilt per facade, and its methods are bound to that facade.\n7\t- Delivery: install_lib_selective copies each top-level _lib entry recursively (scripts/install.sh:1520-1556). test_installed_script_packages.py covers _contamination. _contamination_inventory.py ships under the *.py rule (CHANGELOG:342-346).\n8\t- Governance: the new packages fall under the canonical guard \".claude/hooks/_lib/**/*.py\" (_lib/canonical_edit/guards.py:34). They are not kernel, are not reached by kernel code, and condition 5 does not claim them. No hook calls ceo-boot.py, context-budget.py or check_contamination.py, so none of this can block a session.\n9\t- Python 3.9 and stdlib only: OK. tuple[...] appears only in annotations, under \"from __future__ import annotations\", and Path.is_relative_to exists on 3.9.\n10\t- Fail direction: the contamination code is a gate and fails closed on input, consistent with the repo's rule. The GPG verification in the ceremony toolkit fails closed: it needs an explicit keyring, refuses an empty allowlist, rejects EXPKEYSIG/REVKEYSIG because GOODSIG is required, and refuses symlinks.\n11\t- No declared condition is contradicted by this payload. Condition 7 (thin entry points, logic in _lib modules) matches these three scripts.\n12\t\n13\tFINDINGS\n14\tNone at P0. None at P1.\n15\t\n16\tNEW FINDINGS (annex)\n17\tNone.\n18\t\n19\tP2 FOLLOW-UPS\n20\t1. P2 — .claude/hooks/_lib/_ceremony_toolkit/preflight.py:50-62\n21\t   - Defect: _canonical_patterns() looks for a top-level ast.Assign of _CANONICAL_GUARDS in .claude/hooks/check_canonical_edit.py. The guards moved to _lib/canonical_edit/guards.py:5. The only assignment left in check_canonical_edit.py is the fallback \"_CANONICAL_GUARDS = []\" inside the except at :124, which sits inside an ast.Try node and is never visited.\n22\t   - Failure scenario: in the shipped tree, collect_modified_canonical_paths() and validate_sentinel_pack() called without explicit canonical_patterns always return valid=False with reason \"canonical_guard_policy_unavailable\". The docstring \"Read the actual guard policy\" is therefore false. The tests (test_plan188_ceremony_toolkit.py:47,95) always pass explicit